\begin{align*}
\text{Area of } ABYX &= \frac{1}2 \cdot \text{(base)} \cdot \text{(height)} \\
&= \frac{1}2 \cdot 12 \cdot 12 \\
&= 72
\end{align*}

Following a similar method, we can calculate the area of $XYCD$. Since $XD=6$, we have $XD=6$ and $DC=6$, hence $XY = 6$.

\begin{align*}
\text{Area of } XYCD &= \frac{1}2 \cdot \text{(base)} \cdot \text{(height)} \\
&= \frac{1}2 \cdot (10 + 12)\cdot 6 \\
&= 66
\end{align*}

Finally, we calculate the ratio of both areas.

\begin{align*}
\frac{\text{Area of } ABYX}{\text{Area of } XYCD} &= \frac{72}{66} \\
&= \frac{12}{11}
\end{align*}

Converting the ratio to $p:q$, we have $p=12$ and $q=11$. Thus, $p+q = 12 + 11 = \boxed{23}$.

