response:
 To create $n$ rows, we need $s(n)$ unit rods and $c(n)$ connectors to get $d(n)$ pieces. By making one column of $n-2$ rows outsides, we get $s(n) = s(n-1)+s(n-2)$, $c(n) = c(n-1)+c(n-2)-s(n-2)+n-2$ and $d(n)=d(n-1)+\left[\,\begin{matrix}\displaystyle n-1 \\ \displaystyle 2 \quad \end{matrix}\right]$.

Let's compute $s(n)$, $c(n)$ and $d(n)$ for small $n$ to notice a pattern:
$$\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|}\hline
n& s(n)&c(n)&d(n)\\\hline
1&1&0&1\\
2& 2& 1& 3\\
3&3 &3 & 6\\
4 & 5&6&10\\
5 & 8& 9& 15\\
6 &13&14&21\\
7 & 21 & 18& 34\\
8 & 34 & 22 & 50\\
\hline
\end{array}$$

The table for 3 rows is:



[asy]
draw((4,0)--(1,sqrt(3))--(1,-sqrt(3))--cycle,linewidth(1));
markscalefactor=0.1;
draw((4,0)--(1.5,sqrt(3))--(0.5,sqrt(3))--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw((1,0)--(2,sqrt(3))--(0,sqrt(3))--cycle,linewidth(1));
dot((1,0));
dot((2,0));
dot((4,0));
dot((1,sqrt(3)));
dot((3,sqrt(3)));
dot((0,sqrt(3)));
dot((1,0));
dot((1.5,sqrt(3)));
dot((0.5,sqrt(3)));
dot((0,0));
dot((2,sqrt(3)));
dot((0,sqrt(3)));
[/asy]

So we have $s(3)=3$, $c(3)=6$ and $d(3)=12$.



In general, these functions $s$, $c$ and $d$ are the best we can do. $s(n)$, $c(n)$ and $d(n)$ do not have a simple form, but are recorded as A005258, A005259 and A007582 in Sloane's OEIS database.

